// Foir's authorization spec — the input to the Demesne compiler (EID-252).
// This is platform POLICY; the engine that parses/compiles it is platform-
// agnostic. Reconciled against the live RLS + authz.Policy via the oracle-gap
// spike (RFC §8.2 worked instance). The `procedures`/`ungoverned` blocks below
// carry a representative slice; the full ~80/~45-row tables are pinned when the
// PDP emitter + oracle run (they must reproduce authz.Policy/Ungoverned exactly).
//
// End-state: the transitional Foir-isms are gone. EID-263 retired platform
// `sharing` (the `shared`/`parent_shared` cascade → the access descriptor) and
// EID-254 retired the staff god-flag (the old boolean staff column → the scoped
// `impersonation` grant below). Staff are now a grant-scoped subject with
// zero ambient authority.

topology {
  level platform virtual            // synthetic root: NO scope column — staff (grant-scoped) anchor here
  level tenant   parent platform
  level project  parent tenant
  // SaaS-on-Foir: one project per product, one org per the SaaS's own tenant.
  // Defaults to column org_id / claim org_id. Opt-in is PER OBJECT, via
  // `scoped ... > org wildcard`: the user directory, every content table AND
  // the definition tables take it. Configuration is org-aware by COMPOSITION —
  // one project schema plus a per-org overlay, resolved deepest-first — so a
  // definition carries the axis like anything else. An object that has not opted
  // in emits a policy body byte-identical to the three-level tree.
  //
  // The opt-in marker is `wildcard`, not bare `> org`, and that is load-bearing.
  // `org_id = claims->>'org_id'` is NULL — not TRUE — when the claim is absent, so
  // a bare opt-in would hide the project-shared (org_id IS NULL) rows from every
  // caller that carries no org claim. `wildcard` emits a ROW-NULL widening, not a
  // claim-absence widening, so it does not reproduce the records_select fail-open
  // shape (a missing user_id claim WIDENING the reach).
  //
  // `confers select, insert` BOUNDS that widening to the ops that need it, and
  // the two halves say one sentence: you can SEE the shared context you are
  // working within, and what you CHANGE lands where you stand.
  //
  //   select, insert  (org_id IS NULL OR org_id = claims->>'org_id')
  //   update, delete   org_id IS NOT DISTINCT FROM claims->>'org_id'
  //
  // IS NOT DISTINCT FROM is a strict narrowing of the first form: it differs in
  // exactly one cell, where a claim-carrying caller met a NULL row. A caller with
  // no claim at this level still reaches the shared tier on every op, because
  // withdrawing that would confine callers by a level they do not stand in.
  //
  // INSERT keeps the widening rather than joining update and delete, because
  // five opted-in tables deliberately carry NO claim stamp: notifications,
  // notification_preferences and device_tokens pivot on the OWNER's org, and
  // events and event_deliveries on the SUBJECT's. For those the row's org is a
  // fact about something else, not about the writer, so a writer standing in one
  // org legitimately inserts a row carrying another org or none. Narrowing insert
  // would refuse exactly the shared event that a confined credential must emit
  // when it edits a shared record. On the tables that DO stamp, the stamp already
  // forces org_id to the claim, so insert and update agree on behaviour anyway.
  //
  // ── THE POPULATION, AND HOW TO KNOW IT IS STILL THIS ───────────────────────
  //
  // FIFTY-TWO objects opt in, over fifty tables (`models` is two
  // objects, `models` and `model_reach`, and `apps` is two, `apps` and
  // `app_install`). `user_standing` reads `memberships` on the axis for the
  // users object to borrow; the `memberships` object itself is the registry of
  // who is in an organisation and is confined the way the registry is, as
  // `join_requests`, the requests to be in one, is.
  // Sixty-eight do not. That partition IS the population any organisation reach
  // applies to, and it is not written out
  // here: an earlier note in this file said TWENTY, which was true when written
  // and had silently doubled, and a second hand-copied list is the same bug
  // waiting. `TestOrgAxisPopulationIsPinned` derives the set from THIS file and
  // fails with both sides printed, so the list lives in one place and cannot go
  // quietly wrong. Read it there.
  //
  // ── THE CLOSURE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // Organisations nest. `organization_closure` carries the ancestry, maintained
  // by trigger from `organizations.parent_id`, with RLS forced and ZERO policies
  // — nobody reads it directly, because a direct read would hand over a whole
  // project's tree in one query and a direct write would BE a containment grant.
  // Every access is through the SECURITY DEFINER kernel.
  //
  // Two directions, and they are not symmetrical:
  //
  //   rows reach DOWN        `organization_closure_reach_set`   an ancestor's
  //                          caller sees its descendants' rows
  //   definitions inherit UP `organization_closure_inherit_set` an org resolves
  //                          a definition its ancestor declared
  //
  // The second is `reach ancestry via inheritance for select` on an object, and
  // it is select-only by construction: inheriting a definition is reading one,
  // and an org that could WRITE its ancestor's definition would be editing every
  // sibling's configuration.
  //
  // ── WHAT DOES NOT TAKE THE AXIS, AND WHY ──────────────────────────────────
  //
  // Four classes, each for a different reason, and none of them "not done yet":
  //
  //   the REGISTRY        organizations, organization_members,
  //                       organization_limits, organization_provisions — these
  //                       DESCRIBE orgs rather than living in one. See their
  //                       own block.
  //   TENANT-level        billing, invitations, impersonation grants,
  //                       project_limits — one tier above a project, so an org
  //                       is two tiers away and has no claim on them.
  //   PER-PERSON          threads, thread_messages, agent_attachments,
  //                       mcp_connections — contained
  //                       by the person who owns the row, not by any tier. See
  //                       the thread object.
  //   PLATFORM-level      the platform's users, sessions, app listings, billing plans —
  //                       no tenant, so no org.
  //
  // A fifth class would be "carries org_id but confines in the handler rather
  // than the policy": api_keys, relying_parties, custom_domains,
  // invitations, role_assignments and the two billing usage tables.
  // Those are a KNOWN GAP rather than a refusal, filed on TTS-2293's comment
  // trail, and they are the reason this list is worth pinning — a caller-side
  // filter leaks the moment a new surface forgets it.
  level org      parent project
}

